 "notes": "SAP OITT/ITT1 sub-BOMs on purchased items are read as an in-house make route. Make cost is SAP standard/last-purchase economics — preform price plus conversion rate — not a measured blowing-line cost.",
 "caveats": [
  "Delivery dates are weak in this data: the SAP ship date equals the order date on 2511 of 2645 open lines, so age is order age and not a confirmed missed date.",
  "A purchase order counts as supply here only if its date is in the future or slipped 30 days or less. The open PO book is heavily stale — 797 overdue lines, 448 of them over 180 days — so on SAP's own 'all open POs' basis the buy list would be smaller, and wrong.",
  "Intercompany lines (166, 56,371,420 INR) are the group selling to itself. The EXTERNAL scope drops them so the same litres are not planned twice; the ALL scope keeps them.",
  "Stock is pooled across the Oil and Mart warehouses, because a Mart order Mart can fill never reaches the factory. Work orders, components, purchase orders and lead times are Oil's alone — Oil is the only production unit.",
  "The two company databases number their items independently, so a Mart order is matched to the factory's item by NAME and only accepted on the code when both masters agree. Matching on the code alone would plan the wrong product entirely.",
  "Make-vs-buy is SAP BOM economics. The factory blowing module holds no runs and no preform specs, so it is not a measured blowing-line cost.",
  "31 item(s) on 455 line(s), worth 32,375,903 INR, could not be matched to anything the factory makes. They are listed in full and left out of the production gap — not counted as covered.",
  "9 SKU(s) with a production gap have no bill of materials in SAP, so nothing was exploded for them and their components are missing from the buy list.",
  "Machine capacities and the SKU-to-machine map are not on file. Upload the JIVO Supply Chain Reference Template to turn the production gap into a feasibility check."
 ],
